and **Tornadoes of 2011** |. On the afternoon of April 27, 2011, a large, long-tracked, and destructive EF3 tornado, known as the **Sawyerville–Eoline tornado**, moved across Central 
Alabama, in the United States, moving through areas near numerous towns along its 72.1-mile (116.0 km) track, including Tishabee, Sawyerville, Havana and Eoline. It occurred as part 
of the largest tornado outbreak in modern history, and was one of 21 EF3-rated tornadoes to be confirmed that day. Damage was inflicted to several structures and trees as the tornado 
moved into the Sawyerville area, where EF3 damage was inflicted to more structures. The tornado maintained a wide and long swath of EF3 intensity at its center as it hit Eoline.", 
