  ],
  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "National Weather Service Birmingham’s storm survey for the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado on April 27, 2011 describes a long-track, strong tornado affecting 
Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties in central Alabama. The survey rates the tornado as EF-3, with estimated maximum winds of 145 mph, a path length of 72.13 miles, and a maximum width of
1760 yards (about 1 mile). It reports 50 injuries and 7 fatalities. The tornado is documented as beginning in far southwest Greene County near the Tombigbee River just west of 
Tishabee on County Road 69 near Head Drive around 5:30 pm at coordinates 32.6152 / -88.0543 and ending near Marvel in Bibb County around 6:55 pm at 33.1524 / -86.9898. The narrative 
states that the tornado’s damage intensity increased along its path: it started as EF-1 in Greene County, strengthened to EF-2 there, then intensified to EF-3 north of Sawyerville in 
Hale County near Alabama Route 14 with 145 mph winds, causing extensive structural damage, numerous destroyed mobile and single-family homes, damage to at least one church, thousands 
of downed trees, about 40 injuries, and 6 fatalities in that area. The tornado crossed multiple roads and communities, including County Roads 18, 21, and 29, Alabama Highway 69 south 
of Harper Hill, and areas east of Ingram, and then moved into the Talladega National Forest producing significant tree damage up to the Bibb County line. Continuing northeast, it 
moved through Eoline in Bibb County, where EF-3-level structural damage occurred, destroying numerous mobile homes, single-family homes, the Eoline Fire Department, and another 
business; a vehicle-related fatality and at least 10 injuries occurred near Eoline. The survey notes that the tornado weakened to EF-2 with 125 mph winds as it crossed Alabama Highway
5 south of West Blocton near County Road 26, where several structures sustained major damage or were destroyed, then continued to weaken and lifted just northeast of Marvel. The 
report emphasizes that tornado warnings were in effect for Greene, Hale, and Bibb Counties from 4:50 pm until 7:45 pm and includes links to radar imagery and other supporting 
graphics.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The National Weather Service Birmingham’s “Historic Outbreak of April 27, 2011” page summarizes a major tornado outbreak that affected central Alabama as 
part of a broader multi-day severe weather episode from April 25–28, 2011 across the eastern United States. It characterizes the event as historic in scope and impact, provides an 
event summary for central Alabama, and includes tabular listings of the many tornadoes that occurred in the Birmingham County Warning Area, including the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado. 
The overview describes that this outbreak was associated with a large-scale weather system that produced numerous strong and violent tornadoes over several days, and it links to 
additional resources, including a NOAA GeoPlatform page dedicated to April 27, 2011 in Alabama. The page offers contextual statistics and descriptions of the overall number, strength,
and distribution of tornadoes in central Alabama during the outbreak, situating individual events like the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado within the broader regional sequence of storms.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The National Weather Service Mobile/Pensacola’s page on the April 27, 2011 tornado outbreak describes a major tornado outbreak across the Deep South that 
took place during a three-day period from April 25–28, 2011. It notes that this outbreak was one of the largest, deadliest, and most destructive tornado outbreaks in U.S. history. The
page states that there were 343 tornadoes over the three days, with 207 tornadoes occurring on April 27 alone, and that four of the April 27 tornadoes reached EF-5 intensity. It 
explains that only one of the 207 tornadoes occurred within the Mobile/Pensacola County Warning Area on April 27, an EF-3 that crossed into northwestern Choctaw County, Alabama. The 
page references Storm Prediction Center severe weather archives and supercell rotation track analyses from the National Severe Storms Laboratory to illustrate the geographic extent of
the outbreak and how far south the tornado-producing storms extended. It provides a detailed case study of the Northern Choctaw County EF-3 tornado, including its timing, path across 
multiple NWS County Warning Areas, track length of about 23.7 miles, and maximum winds of approximately 150 mph, highlighting how long-lived supercells produced multiple strong and 
violent tornadoes over a wide region.",
      "source_ref": "s3"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d4",
      "document_summary": "The Wikipedia article on the 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado describes a large, long-tracked, destructive EF3 tornado that moved across central Alabama on 
the afternoon of April 27, 2011. It states that the tornado traveled about 72.1 miles, affecting areas near towns such as Tishabee, Sawyerville, Havana, and Eoline. The article 
situates this tornado within what it calls the largest tornado outbreak in modern history and includes sections on meteorological synopsis, a detailed tornado summary, and references 
to National Weather Service pages, Storm Prediction Center data, and news reports. As a secondary, collaboratively edited source, it synthesizes information from multiple primary 
references rather than providing original observational data.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "The WeatherAPI.com page labeled as providing weather information for Sawyerville, Alabama returns real-time or recent weather data in JSON format, including
fields for location (such as name, region, and country) and current conditions like temperature in degrees Celsius and qualitative descriptions such as 'Partly cloudy'. This content 
pertains to contemporary routine weather conditions rather than historical severe weather events and does not address the April 27, 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado.",
      "source_ref": "s5"
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
