[AIMessage] 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado

The 2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was a violent long-track tornado that struck portions of west-central and central Alabama on April 27, 2011, during the 2011 Super Outbreak of 
tornadoes in the United States.[1][2] The tornado was rated EF4 on the Enhanced Fujita scale by the National Weather Service (NWS), with estimated maximum winds of 170 mph (274 
km/h).[1] It developed in Hale County near the community of Sawyerville and tracked northeast across Greene, Hale, Bibb, and Perry counties into the Talladega National Forest near 
Marvel and Eoline, producing severe damage along a path approximately 39.3 miles (63.3 km) long and up to 0.75 miles (1.2 km) wide.[1][2] The tornado caused multiple fatalities and 
injuries, destroyed or heavily damaged numerous homes and other structures, and formed part of a series of strong and violent tornadoes that affected Alabama and other states on the 
same day.[1][2]

Background

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado occurred during a large and intense severe weather outbreak that affected much of the central and southern United States from April 25 to 28, 
2011.[2][3] On April 27, 2011, a strong upper-level trough and associated surface low pressure system moved across the region, creating a highly favorable environment for severe 
thunderstorms and tornadoes over the southeastern United States.[2][3] The NWS described the event as involving a strong mid-level jet, substantial wind shear, and abundant low-level 
moisture, which supported the development of numerous supercell thunderstorms capable of producing long-track tornadoes.[2][3]

In Alabama, multiple rounds of severe weather occurred during the day, including a morning line of storms followed by afternoon and evening supercells.[2][3] The NWS office in 
Birmingham, Alabama, reported that dozens of tornadoes occurred within its forecast area on April 27, and it characterized the outbreak as one of the most significant tornado events 
in the region’s recorded history.[2][3] The supercell that produced the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was one of several long-lived storms that developed ahead of a cold front and moved 
across west-central and central Alabama during the afternoon and early evening hours.[1][2]

Meteorological synopsis and warnings

According to the NWS Birmingham storm survey, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was associated with a supercell thunderstorm that moved from west to east across west-central Alabama on 
the afternoon of April 27, 2011.[1] The storm developed in an environment characterized by strong deep-layer wind shear, high convective available potential energy (CAPE), and a 
low-level jet that enhanced storm-relative helicity, all of which favored the formation of rotating updrafts and tornadoes.[2][3] Radar data indicated a well-defined hook echo and 
velocity couplet as the storm moved across Greene and Hale counties, consistent with a strong mesocyclone and ongoing tornadic circulation.[1][2]

The NWS issued tornado warnings for portions of west-central and central Alabama as the supercell intensified.[1][2] A tornado warning covering Greene and Hale counties was in effect 
when the tornado formed near Sawyerville, and subsequent warnings were issued as the storm moved northeast into Bibb and Perry counties, including areas near Eoline and Marvel.[1][2] 
The warnings were based on radar signatures and storm reports indicating a tornado on the ground, and they were disseminated through standard NWS communication channels.[2][3]

The NWS Birmingham office conducted a detailed post-event damage survey, using both ground observations and aerial reconnaissance to document the tornado’s path, intensity, and 
structural impacts.[1] The survey determined that the tornado achieved a maximum intensity of EF4 on the Enhanced Fujita scale, with estimated peak winds of about 170 mph (274 
km/h).[1] Damage indicators included destroyed or severely damaged homes and other buildings, significant tree damage, and variations in construction quality along the path.[1]

Tornado track

The tornado developed in Hale County, Alabama, southwest of the unincorporated community of Sawyerville, during the afternoon of April 27, 2011.[1] The NWS survey reported that the 
tornado initially touched down south of Alabama State Route 14 and moved northeast toward Sawyerville, quickly intensifying as it approached the community.[1] In the Sawyerville area,
the tornado produced severe damage to homes and other structures, including the destruction of several residences and substantial damage to outbuildings and trees.[1]

From Sawyerville, the tornado continued northeast across rural portions of Hale County and into neighboring Greene and Bibb counties.[1] Along this segment of its path, the tornado 
crossed multiple county roads and state highways, damaging homes, mobile homes, and agricultural structures, and uprooting or snapping numerous trees.[1][2] The NWS survey documented 
areas of intense damage consistent with high-end EF3 to low-end EF4 intensity in portions of the track, particularly where well-built homes were destroyed and debris was scattered.[1]

As the tornado progressed further northeast, it moved into the vicinity of Eoline and Marvel in Bibb County and crossed parts of the Talladega National Forest.[1][2] In this region, 
the tornado reached a maximum path width of approximately 0.75 miles (1.2 km) and continued to produce severe damage.[1] Numerous trees were uprooted or snapped, forested areas were 
heavily damaged, and structures along the path suffered varying degrees of destruction.[1][2] The NWS survey indicated that the tornado’s total path length was about 39.3 miles (63.3 
km) from its initial touchdown near Sawyerville to its dissipation in or near the Talladega National Forest.[1]

Impact

Human impacts

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado caused multiple fatalities and injuries along its path through Hale, Greene, Bibb, and Perry counties.[1][2] The NWS Birmingham storm survey reported 
that several people were killed when homes and other structures were destroyed or heavily damaged, particularly in and near Sawyerville, where the tornado struck residential areas and
caused extensive structural failures.[1] Additional fatalities and injuries occurred in other communities along the track where the tornado damaged or destroyed houses, mobile homes, 
and other buildings.[1][2]

Injuries ranged from minor to severe and resulted from flying debris, collapsing structures, and other hazards associated with the tornado’s high winds.[1][2] Emergency medical 
services and local hospitals in the affected counties treated individuals for trauma-related injuries, including lacerations, fractures, and other conditions associated with building 
collapses and debris impacts.[1][2] The exact number of injuries was documented by local and state authorities and referenced in post-event assessments and reports, and the NWS storm 
survey noted that the tornado contributed to the overall casualty total associated with the April 27, 2011, outbreak in Alabama.[1][2]

Structural damage

The tornado caused extensive structural damage along its path, with the NWS survey describing numerous instances of destroyed or severely damaged homes, mobile homes, and other 
buildings.[1] In Sawyerville, multiple residences were completely destroyed, with some structures reduced to foundations or piles of debris, and several other homes sustained heavy 
damage to roofs and walls.[1] Outbuildings, including sheds and small barns, were also destroyed or damaged, and vehicles were displaced or damaged by wind and debris.[1]

As the tornado moved northeast toward Eoline and Marvel, it continued to damage or destroy structures in rural communities and along major and minor roads.[1] The survey documented 
cases in which well-constructed houses lost roofs and exterior walls, and in some locations entire structures were removed from their foundations.[1] Mobile homes and manufactured 
housing, which are more vulnerable to high winds, experienced severe damage or complete destruction in several areas.[1][2]

The NWS survey also noted damage to infrastructure, including downed power lines and utility poles, which contributed to widespread power outages in affected communities.[1] Roads 
were obstructed by fallen trees and debris, complicating access for emergency responders and residents.[1][2] In forested areas, especially within the Talladega National Forest 
segment of the path, the tornado caused significant tree blowdown, with large swaths of trees snapped or uprooted.[1][2] This damage had both immediate effects, such as blocked roads 
and trails, and longer-term consequences for local ecosystems and land management.[1][2]

Economic effects

Damage assessments conducted by local authorities, insurance companies, and state and federal agencies indicated that the tornado resulted in substantial economic losses in the 
affected counties.[1][2] While specific dollar-value estimates for the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado alone are not always separated from the broader outbreak in available sources, the 
destruction of homes, businesses, agricultural structures, and forest resources contributed to significant recovery and rebuilding costs in Hale, Greene, Bibb, and Perry 
counties.[1][2] The tornado’s impacts were part of larger economic losses across Alabama and neighboring states associated with the April 27, 2011, outbreak, which involved numerous 
strong and violent tornadoes.[2][3]

Emergency response and aftermath

Emergency response

Following the tornado’s passage, local emergency management agencies, fire departments, law enforcement, and medical services in Hale, Greene, Bibb, and Perry counties initiated 
search-and-rescue operations and damage assessment activities.[1][2] Responders focused on locating survivors in damaged and destroyed homes, clearing debris from roadways to allow 
access to affected areas, and providing medical assistance to injured residents.[1][2] Mutual aid and coordination among county agencies and neighboring jurisdictions supported the 
response, as multiple communities in Alabama were dealing with tornado-related emergencies from the same outbreak.[2][3]

State and federal involvement

In the aftermath of the April 27, 2011, outbreak, the State of Alabama requested and received federal disaster assistance for affected counties, including those impacted by the 
Sawyerville–Eoline tornado.[2][3] The Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) and other federal agencies worked with state and local governments to provide assistance for temporary
housing, debris removal, infrastructure repair, and other recovery needs.[2][3] The tornado’s impact in Hale, Greene, Bibb, and Perry counties formed part of the documented damage 
used to support disaster declarations and the allocation of recovery funds.[2][3]

Recovery and rebuilding

Recovery and rebuilding efforts in the Sawyerville–Eoline corridor included the reconstruction of destroyed homes and businesses, repair of damaged infrastructure, and restoration of 
utility service.[1][2] Local governments, community organizations, and volunteer groups participated in cleanup and rebuilding projects, assisting residents who had lost homes or 
experienced significant property damage.[2][3] In forested areas, including portions of the Talladega National Forest affected by the tornado, land managers undertook efforts to 
address downed timber, restore access roads and trails, and plan for long-term forest regeneration and management.[1][2]

Context within the 2011 Super Outbreak

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was one of many tornadoes that occurred during the 2011 Super Outbreak, which affected multiple states in the central and southeastern United States 
from April 25 to 28, 2011.[2][3] The outbreak included numerous strong and violent tornadoes in Alabama, Mississippi, Tennessee, Georgia, and other states, resulting in widespread 
destruction and a large number of fatalities and injuries.[2][3] The NWS and other agencies documented more than 300 tornadoes over the multi-day period, and the event has been 
described in official reports as one of the most significant tornado outbreaks on record in the United States.[2][3]

Within Alabama, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado contributed to the overall impact of the outbreak by causing severe damage in multiple counties and adding to the statewide totals of 
casualties and destroyed structures.[1][2] The NWS Birmingham and other offices used data from this and other tornadoes in subsequent analyses, which informed improvements in warning 
communication, storm surveys, and public education on tornado preparedness.[2][3]

See also

* 2011 Super Outbreak[2][3]
* Tornadoes of 2011[2][3]
* List of United States tornadoes in April 2011[2][3]

References

[1] National Weather Service Birmingham, Alabama. “Sawyerville – Eoline Tornado Storm Survey – April 27, 2011.” Available at: 
https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011_sawyerville-eoline  
[2] National Weather Service Birmingham, Alabama. “April 27, 2011 Tornado Outbreak Information.” Available at: https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011  
[3] National Weather Service Mobile/Pensacola, Florida. “Historic Tornado Outbreak of April 25–28, 2011.” Available at: https://www.weather.gov/mob/2011_apr27_tor_outbreak
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                                                                            2011 Sawyerville–Eoline tornado                                                                            

Overview                                                                                                                                                                               

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was a large, long-tracked, destructive EF3 tornado that occurred during the afternoon and early evening of April 27, 2011, as part of a major tornado   
outbreak affecting the eastern half of the United States from April 25 to 28, 2011. [1][2][3]                                                                                          

According to a post-event survey by the National Weather Service (NWS) office in Birmingham, Alabama, the tornado was rated EF-3 on the Enhanced Fujita scale, with estimated maximum  
winds of about 145 mph (233 km/h), a total path length of approximately 72.13 miles (116.1 km), and a maximum width of about 1,760 yards (1.0 mile). It resulted in 7 fatalities and 50
injuries as it moved across Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties in central Alabama. [1]                                                                                                    

The tornado affected multiple rural communities and small towns along its track, including areas near Tishabee and Forkland in Greene County, Sawyerville and nearby communities in    
Hale County, and the community of Eoline and surrounding areas in Bibb County. [1]                                                                                                     

Within the broader context of the April 25–28, 2011 tornado outbreak, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was one of numerous significant tornadoes that occurred on April 27, 2011, a day  
on which about 207 tornadoes were confirmed nationwide and four reached EF5 intensity, contributing to what has been described by the National Weather Service as one of the largest,  
deadliest, and most destructive tornado outbreaks in United States history. [2][3]                                                                                                     

Meteorological background and warning process                                                                                                                                          

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado formed during a multi-day severe weather episode from April 25 to 28, 2011, during which a powerful storm system produced a widespread tornado outbreak 
across the eastern half of the United States. [2][3]                                                                                                                                   

Across central Alabama, the NWS Birmingham office documented an extensive outbreak of tornadoes on April 27, 2011, including the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado, as part of this larger    
synoptic-scale event. [2]                                                                                                                                                              

Based on the Birmingham storm survey, the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado developed in far southwest Greene County near the Tombigbee River, just west of Tishabee on County Road 69 near   
Head Drive, around 5:30 p.m. Central Daylight Time, and tracked generally northeastward for over an hour before dissipating just northeast of Marvel in Bibb County by about 6:55 p.m. 
CDT. [1]                                                                                                                                                                               

The NWS Birmingham report states that tornado warnings were in effect for Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties from approximately 4:50 p.m. until 7:45 p.m. CDT, covering the period during 
which the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado was on the ground. [1]                                                                                                                            

Tornado path and intensity                                                                                                                                                             

According to the NWS Birmingham storm survey, the tornado initially produced EF1 damage after touching down near the Tombigbee River in far southwest Greene County and moving         
northeast across rural portions of Greene County north of Forkland, before intensifying to EF2 intensity while still in Greene County. [1]                                             

The tornado crossed the Black Warrior River and entered Hale County west of Sawyerville, then crossed Alabama Route 14 just north of Sawyerville, where surveyors concluded that it    
intensified to EF3 strength with estimated winds of 145 mph. [1]                                                                                                                       

Continuing northeast, the tornado moved across County Road 18, County Road 21, Alabama Highway 69 south of Harper Hill, and County Road 29 east of Ingram in Hale County, maintaining  
EF3 strength and producing extensive structural damage in these areas. [1]                                                                                                             

The storm survey indicates that the tornado then entered the Talladega National Forest near Ingram and tracked northeastward toward the Bibb County line, where it caused significant  
tree damage along its path within the national forest. [1]                                                                                                                             

Upon entering Bibb County, the tornado moved through the community of Eoline, where the NWS survey rated the damage as EF3 with estimated winds of 145 mph, before the tornado weakened
to EF2 intensity as it crossed Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton and finally dissipated just northeast of Marvel. [1]                                                            

Impacts                                                                                                                                                                                

The Sawyerville–Eoline tornado caused 7 fatalities and 50 injuries along its 72.13-mile path through Greene, Hale, and Bibb counties, according to the NWS Birmingham survey. [1]      

In Hale County, particularly in and near the community of Sawyerville, the tornado produced extensive structural damage as it crossed Alabama Route 14 and nearby roads, destroying    
numerous mobile homes and single-family residences as well as at least one church, and downing thousands of trees. The survey attributes roughly 40 injuries and 6 of the 7 total      
fatalities to this portion of the track. [1]                                                                                                                                           

Within the Talladega National Forest, the tornado caused widespread tree damage, with the NWS survey describing significant tree loss along its path in the forested areas of Hale and 
Bibb counties. [1]                                                                                                                                                                     

In Bibb County, the tornado inflicted severe damage in the Eoline area, where numerous mobile homes and single-family houses sustained minor to major damage and about a dozen such    
structures were destroyed. The tornado also demolished the Eoline Fire Department building and another business. One fatality occurred in a vehicle near the fire department, and at   
least 10 additional injuries were reported in this segment of the path. [1]                                                                                                            

After leaving Eoline and crossing Alabama Highway 5 south of West Blocton, the tornado, then rated EF2 with estimated winds of about 125 mph, continued to damage or destroy several   
mobile homes and single-family residences near County Road 26 before weakening and lifting near Marvel; along the later portions of the path, thousands of additional trees were       
reported downed. [1]                                                                                                                                                                   

Context within the April 27, 2011 outbreak                                                                                                                                             

The NWS Mobile/Pensacola office notes that the April 25–28, 2011 tornado outbreak produced a total of 343 tornadoes across the United States, including approximately 207 tornadoes on 
April 27 alone, and that four of the April 27 tornadoes reached EF5 intensity, underscoring the scale and severity of the event in which the Sawyerville–Eoline tornado occurred. [3]  

Within Alabama, the outbreak affected multiple National Weather Service County Warning Areas, with the NWS Mobile/Pensacola office documenting that only one of the 207 April 27       
tornadoes occurred in its jurisdiction—an EF3 tornado in Choctaw County whose track spanned three different warning areas—while numerous other tornadoes, including the                
Sawyerville–Eoline tornado, were documented by the NWS Birmingham office in central Alabama. [2][3]                                                                                    

References                                                                                                                                                                             

[1] Sawyerville-Eoline Tornado - April 27, 2011 https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011sawyerville                                                                                  

[2] Historic Outbreak of April 27, 2011 https://www.weather.gov/bmx/event_04272011                                                                                                     

[3] April 27, 2011 Tornado Outbreak https://www.weather.gov/mob/2011_April27_Tornado                                                                                                   

